#!/usr/bin/env python3
# ruff: noqa: E501
"""
elan-swipe -- capture a SWIPE from an ELAN 04f3:0c6e and assemble it exactly
the way upstream libfprint's "elan" driver would, without needing a rebuilt
libfprint and without libfprint's ELAN_MAX_FRAMES=30 ceiling.

    sudo ./elan-swipe                 # capture a swipe, dump everything, analyse
    sudo ./elan-swipe capture -n 200  # capture up to 200 frames
    ./elan-swipe assemble DIR         # re-assemble an existing dump, no device

Why this exists
---------------
04f3:0c6e is 150 x 52 px at ~500 dpi = 7.6 mm x 2.6 mm. That is swipe geometry,
not press geometry. Upstream libfprint declares FP_SCAN_TYPE_SWIPE for it
(drivers/elan.c:1018) and reconstructs a tall image with
fpi_do_movement_estimation + fpi_assemble_frames. Nobody has ever captured a
swipe on this unit, so this tool captures the raw frame stream *and* runs a
bit-faithful Python port of libfprint's assembler over it, so the two can be
judged separately.

Everything the analyser prints about the assembly is a faithful re-execution of
libfprint 1.94.100 fpi-assembling.c. Nothing here claims a match accuracy;
there is no matcher in this file at all.

Device access is raw usbdevfs ioctls (ctypes) so the only dependencies are
numpy and, optionally, Pillow. Requires root for /dev/bus/usb write access.
"""

import argparse
import ctypes
import errno
import json
import os
import struct
import subprocess
import sys
import time
from pathlib import Path

import numpy as np

VID, PID = 0x04F3, 0x0C6E

